main.cpp
--cpp11
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ first constructor template: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.2\] line \d+ second constructor template: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.3\] line \d+ WRONG \(must FAIL\): FAILURE$
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^warning: ignoring
^CONVERSION ERROR$
dropped the body
--
N5008 [temp.mem]/1, [over.match.funcs.general]/7: a constructor template
is a template; its specializations are overload candidates, and
[temp.deduct.general]/8 removes those whose (default) template arguments
fail to substitute.  The class-body pass that type-checks constructors
also turned each constructor template into an ordinary constructor; for
one with no function parameters that is a non-template default
constructor, preferred over every template ([over.match.best]/2.4),
built from whichever same-signature template came last.  So S<int>
used the second template's body although its enable_if fails for int.
libstdc++ pair has exactly these two default constructors (implicit and
explicit, constrained on __is_implicitly_default_constructible).  Fails
on the round-163 build bin52.
